## Why This Is Asked
This tests building secure, observable SAP on AWS with automated incident response across accounts.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account data collection and IAM trust
- Kinesis Firehose + S3/Redshift storage
- Anomaly detection with SageMaker or Glue
- Event-driven remediation via Lambda + EventBridge
- Auditing and immutable logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal CDK-like snippet for a cross-account data sink
import * as iam from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-iam';
import * as kinesis from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-kinesis';
import * as cdk from 'aws-cdk-lib';
class SapObservabilityStack extends cdk.Stack {
  constructor(scope, id, props) {
    super(scope, id, props);
    const stream = new kinesis.Stream(this, 'SapAccessStream', { shardCount: 1 });
    stream.grantPutRecords(new iam.AccountRootPrincipal());
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the anomaly model without impacting SAP availability?
- What are the trade-offs of SageMaker vs CloudWatch for this workload?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses incident response capabilities, cross-account IAM architecture, and PrivateLink security in a realistic SAP on AWS deployment scenario. It tests practical knowledge of defense-in-depth strategies and automation.

## Key Concepts
- Tag-based IAM with per-object SAP authorization mapping for granular access revocation
- PrivateLink isolation to sever network connectivity to SAP components during incidents
- KMS key rotation for cryptographic integrity after potential credential exposure
- S3 Object Lock for immutable audit trails ensuring forensic integrity
- Cross-account emergency role design for incident response delegation

## Code Example
```hcl
# Cross-account emergency role (Terraform)
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_emergency_role" {
  name = "SAP-Emergency-Responder"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow"
      Principal = {
        AWS = "arn:aws:iam::${var.security_account_id}:root"
      }
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

# VPC Endpoint Service for SAP components
resource "aws_vpc_endpoint_service" "sap_internal" {
  acceptance_required        = false
  network_load_balancer_arns = [aws_lb.sap_internal.arn]
  tags = {
    Purpose = "SAP-Component-Isolation"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment workflow without triggering actual disruptions?
- How do you ensure the audit snapshot cannot be tampered with by a compromised administrator?
- What metrics would you capture to measure mean-time-to-containment (MTTC)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner's ability to design a practical, auditable maintenance workflow using familiar AWS services with least-privilege access.

## Key Concepts

- AWS Systems Manager Run Command
- IAM roles with trust relationships and least privilege
- CloudWatch Logs and CloudTrail for audit trails
- AWS SSO integration for role assumption
- Minimal IaC (Terraform) to create roles and SSM associations

## Code Example

```terraform
# Terraform snippet creating cross-account SAPOperator role and IAM policy
provider "aws" { region = "us-east-1" }

# Role
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator" {
  name = "SAPOperator"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow",
      Principal = { AWS = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:saml-provider/AWS_SSO" },
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

# Policy
resource "aws_iam_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "SAPOperatorPolicy"
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["ssm:SendCommand","ssm:ListCommands","ssm:DescribeInstanceInformation"], Resource = "*" },
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["logs:CreateLogGroup","logs:CreateLogStream","logs:PutLogEvents"], Resource = "*" }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "attach" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.sap_operator.name
  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.sap_operator_policy.arn
}
```

```terraform
# SSM association to run healthcheck on SAP EC2
data "aws_ssm_document" "run_shell" { name = "AWS-RunShellScript" }

resource "aws_ssm_association" "healthcheck" {
  name            = data.aws_ssm_document.run_shell.name
  instance_id     = "<SAP_EC2_INSTANCE_ID>"
  parameters = {
    commands = ["bash /usr/local/bin/healthcheck.sh"]
  }
}
```

